-- config/border.lua — INERT. Do not re-append this file.
--
-- This used to draw the titlebar accent rule. It was removed on 2026-09-27,
-- and this tombstone replaces the code that drew it. Nothing here is loaded:
-- `grep -rn "config\.border" --include='*.lua' .` finds only comments, and
-- wezterm.lua has no `:append(require('config.border'))`.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE IS STILL HERE
--
-- Deleting it is not a safe cleanup. config/init.lua resolves a duplicate key
-- FIRST-WINS and only logs a warning, so the moment this file is appended
-- anywhere ahead of config/appearance.lua it wins `window_decorations`:
--
--     config/border.lua:27    window_decorations = 'TITLE',
--     config/appearance.lua:99  window_decorations = 'NONE',
--
-- and the titlebar comes back with a clean diff, one log line, and no error.
-- The old version of this file made that worse by telling the reader
-- "Removing the border = delete one :append line" — advice that re-arms a
-- dead file the moment anyone follows it.
--
-- So: an inert file that says why beats a deleted file that says nothing. It
-- is superseded by config/appearance.lua, which owns the key.
--
-- WHAT TO DO INSTEAD
--
--   * Want the accent rule back? Write it in config/appearance.lua, where the
--     key already lives. Do not restore this file.
--   * Want the border GONE for good? Then this file can be deleted, and
--     tests/check_border.lua's assertion that nothing writes 'TITLE' becomes
--     a permanent guard on that.
--   * Editing the comment block below is safe; adding code is not, unless you
--     also remove this warning.
--
-- Checked by tests/check_border.lua, which asserts on the VALUE rather than
-- on this file's name -- rename this file or inline it and the guard holds.

return {}
